1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,656
Professionalism is observable behaviour. A patient sees it when you
introduce yourself, ask permission, explain an examination and cover
them again afterwards.

2
00:00:10,656 --> 00:00:22,872
Integrity means recording the findings you actually obtained. If a
measurement was not completed, state that and explain why, rather than
filling the gap with an assumed normal result.

3
00:00:22,872 --> 00:00:35,280
Reliability includes checking equipment before a call, completing
agreed tasks and noticing when the original plan is no longer safe.
Courtesy without dependable clinical work is not enough.

4
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:44,448
Competence includes asking for assistance. A professional identifies a
limit before that limit exposes the patient to avoidable harm.

5
00:00:44,448 --> 00:00:55,512
In British Columbia, the Emergency Medical Assistants Licensing Board
is the regulator. BCEHS is a service and employer; it is not another
name for the Licensing Board.

6
00:00:55,512 --> 00:01:05,784
The regulation defines licence categories, authorized services and
endorsements. A particular licence can also have conditions or
restrictions.

7
00:01:05,784 --> 00:01:20,376
Operational practice must also fit the service's authorization,
available resources and your demonstrated competence. Reading a
procedure or receiving a suggestion from a colleague does not
establish that you are authorized to perform it.

8
00:01:20,376 --> 00:01:28,824
When the limits are unclear, keep providing the safe care you can
deliver and clarify through the appropriate clinical or supervisory
channel.

9
00:01:28,824 --> 00:01:38,520
Imagine a colleague asks you to use a device that you have watched
once but have never practised with. Familiarity with the name is
different from safe performance.

10
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:49,032
Check legal authority, service authorization, your capability and the
patient's needs. These are separate checks; passing one does not
answer the others.

11
00:01:49,032 --> 00:02:00,312
State the limitation plainly: I am not trained to operate this device
independently. I can continue monitoring while we obtain a qualified
operator or clinical advice.

12
00:02:00,312 --> 00:02:08,544
This response offers a safe next action. It avoids both an unsupported
attempt and simply withdrawing from patient care.

13
00:02:08,544 --> 00:02:22,080
Fatigue can slow decisions and make a simple calculation difficult.
Illness, emotional distress, alcohol, other substances and prescribed
medication effects can also impair safe work.

14
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:32,832
Notice functional evidence: repeated missed instructions, inability to
stay awake, an unsafe driving decision or a task you cannot reliably
complete.

15
00:02:32,832 --> 00:02:47,280
Act on the immediate risk. Tell your partner and supervisor, arrange a
safe handover of the task and follow the service's process. Concealing
impairment to avoid inconveniencing a team transfers risk to the
patient.

16
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:59,016
If the concern involves a colleague, describe the behaviour you
observed rather than diagnosing its cause. Immediate protection and a
factual report are more useful than an accusation.

17
00:02:59,016 --> 00:03:10,176
Accountability means being answerable for your assessment, decisions,
actions and omissions. It continues through treatment, documentation
and the transfer of care.

18
00:03:10,176 --> 00:03:20,280
You should be able to explain what you knew at the time, what you did,
why you chose it and what changed afterwards. Hindsight alone should
not replace this actual sequence.

19
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:33,600
Documentation supports accountability when it contains observations,
times, reasoning, actions and responses. It fails when it contains a
polished story that hides a delay or uncertainty.

20
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:44,352
Professional responsibility also applies off duty. A patient story
posted online can identify someone through combined details, even when
the name is removed.

21
00:03:44,352 --> 00:03:54,936
An error response starts with the patient, not with deciding who is at
fault. Assess for harm, obtain the necessary clinical assistance and
maintain continuity of care.

22
00:03:54,936 --> 00:04:06,720
Tell the relevant people what is known, what is uncertain and what has
already been done. Follow the service's disclosure and incident-
reporting process rather than making promises about an outcome.

23
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:17,712
Record clinical facts in the patient record. Use the separate safety-
reporting channel for the event investigation. An incident report does
not replace the patient care report.

24
00:04:17,712 --> 00:04:28,896
A near miss also reveals a weak point. For example, two look-alike
equipment labels deserve a process correction even if a final check
prevented the wrong item from being used.

25
00:04:28,896 --> 00:04:39,984
Suppose a partner starts entering an unmeasured blood pressure. A
useful challenge names the specific action and the patient-safety
concern, then asks for a correction.

26
00:04:39,984 --> 00:04:51,024
If the concern is not resolved, escalate to the appropriate supervisor
or clinical support. Do not let hierarchy turn an invented finding
into a permanent patient record.

27
00:04:51,024 --> 00:05:05,904
The BC Code of Ethics includes reporting incompetent, illegal or
unethical conduct to appropriate authorities. The destination and
urgency depend on the concern; gossip and public shaming are not
reporting systems.

28
00:05:05,904 --> 00:05:19,296
Describe observations, exact relevant words, actions taken and
unresolved risks. Keep clinical records factual and use the proper
personnel or regulatory process for conduct concerns.

29
00:05:19,296 --> 00:05:30,816
A clinical relationship contains an unequal distribution of
information, authority and vulnerability. Boundaries prevent the
professional from using that position to meet personal needs.

30
00:05:30,816 --> 00:05:42,288
Compassion means listening and responding to the patient's concern. It
does not require sharing your private problems, offering personal
money or becoming the patient's private contact after a call.

31
00:05:42,288 --> 00:05:54,624
Before physical contact, explain what you need to examine and why. Use
appropriate covering and assistance. A clinically necessary
examination still requires respectful communication.

32
00:05:54,624 --> 00:06:04,080
Watch for thoughts such as this patient needs only me, or I should
hide this favour from my team. Those are reasons to pause and seek
supervision.

33
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:15,816
A dual relationship occurs when your professional role overlaps with a
personal, social or financial role. In a small community, encountering
someone you know may be unavoidable.

34
00:06:15,816 --> 00:06:27,912
The overlap is a risk to manage, not proof that you must abandon
urgent care. Provide necessary help, identify the conflict and arrange
another appropriate clinician when feasible.

35
00:06:27,912 --> 00:06:38,592
Use the same assessment and documentation standards. Do not skip
sensitive questions because you know the family, and do not tell
mutual friends that the patient called for help.

36
00:06:38,592 --> 00:06:50,184
Gifts and special favours can create obligation. Follow the applicable
gift policy, decline inappropriate personal benefits and seek guidance
when the boundary is uncertain.

37
00:06:50,184 --> 00:07:00,288
If a patient asks for your personal phone number, begin by
acknowledging the underlying need. They may be frightened of being
alone or unsure how to access follow-up.

38
00:07:00,288 --> 00:07:10,272
Then state the professional limit clearly and kindly. Offer a service-
based option without promising that a referral will be accepted or
available immediately.

39
00:07:10,272 --> 00:07:20,352
Brief self-disclosure is only useful if it serves the patient's care.
A conversation that asks the patient to comfort you reverses the
professional relationship.

40
00:07:20,352 --> 00:07:30,480
If you feel unusually attached or responsible, discuss that reaction
with an appropriate supervisor or support person while protecting
patient confidentiality.

41
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:42,168
Autonomy asks whose decision this is and whether the person has the
information and opportunity needed to decide. A recommendation should
help the patient make a choice, not silence them.

42
00:07:42,168 --> 00:07:54,240
Beneficence asks what good the proposed action could achieve.
Nonmaleficence asks what harm it might cause, including harm from
delay, coercion or an unnecessary intervention.

43
00:07:54,240 --> 00:08:05,952
Justice asks whether care is fair. Clinical urgency and need can
justify different priorities; wealth, social status or your personal
liking of a patient cannot.

44
00:08:05,952 --> 00:08:16,680
The principles may pull in different directions. Use them to expose
the competing concerns, then explain a defensible action rather than
merely naming a principle.

45
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:28,608
For adult consent in BC, capability is presumed unless the contrary is
demonstrated. Disagreeing with your recommendation does not, by
itself, show incapability.

46
00:08:28,608 --> 00:08:39,936
Explain the condition, the proposed care, important risks and
benefits, and reasonable alternatives. Ask the person to describe what
those options could mean for their own situation.

47
00:08:39,936 --> 00:08:50,520
A capable adult can refuse care, including a choice with serious
consequences. Family anxiety does not automatically transfer the
decision to a relative.

48
00:08:50,520 --> 00:09:05,832
If understanding, voluntariness or a possible emergency exception is
uncertain, obtain appropriate clinical support and follow the consent
pathway. This ethical discussion is not a complete refusal or mental-
health algorithm.

49
00:09:05,832 --> 00:09:17,496
Start by stating the dilemma in one sentence. For example: I recommend
assessment in hospital, but the patient is declining because they fear
leaving a dependent person alone.

50
00:09:17,496 --> 00:09:26,976
Gather clinical findings and ask what matters to the patient. Separate
a confirmed fact from an assumption about their motivation or
capability.

51
00:09:26,976 --> 00:09:40,752
Compare options that are genuinely available. Consider urgent
treatment, further discussion, an interpreter, a support person or
clinical consultation, without inventing resources you cannot arrange.

52
00:09:40,752 --> 00:09:51,528
Choose an action within the applicable authority, explain the trade-
offs and record the reasoning. Reassess if symptoms, wishes or
available information change.

53
00:09:51,528 --> 00:10:02,664
In this original teaching case, the patient wants the recommended
assessment. The immediate obstacle is concern for a dependent
relative, not a settled refusal of medical care.

54
00:10:02,664 --> 00:10:13,032
First assess clinical urgency. Then ask whether an appropriate person
or established service could help with the relative, and whether the
patient agrees to that contact.

55
00:10:13,032 --> 00:10:27,144
Autonomy supports understanding the patient's priorities. Beneficence
supports needed assessment. Nonmaleficence makes us consider harm from
delay, and justice requires a fair response to a social barrier.

56
00:10:27,144 --> 00:10:40,680
A defensible plan combines clinical care with a feasible, consent-
based support step. If no support is available, explain that
limitation, obtain clinical advice when needed and document the
patient's decision.

57
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:52,512
Advocacy means helping a patient access appropriate care or support.
Common barriers include medication access, transport, housing,
language and limited social support.

58
00:10:52,512 --> 00:11:04,464
Ask what is preventing access instead of labelling the person
noncompliant. The difference matters: an unanswered practical barrier
may continue after today's symptoms improve.

59
00:11:04,464 --> 00:11:18,528
Offer choices, obtain the appropriate agreement and use established
referral routes. Ordinary referral consent is not a universal rule for
every legally required disclosure; those duties need their own
pathway.

60
00:11:18,528 --> 00:11:30,168
Do not guarantee acceptance, fund care personally or take over long-
term case management. Effective advocacy connects the patient to a
service while keeping responsibility clear.

61
00:11:30,168 --> 00:11:40,872
Limited English is a communication barrier, not evidence of
incapability. Begin with immediate safety and assessment while
arranging the appropriate language support.

62
00:11:40,872 --> 00:11:52,632
Explain to relatives that you need to hear the patient's own account.
Address the patient, use brief questions and allow interpretation time
rather than speaking only to the interpreter.

63
00:11:52,632 --> 00:12:05,376
For important choices, use the service's appropriate interpreting
process rather than assuming a relative's summary is complete or
neutral. Ask the patient to explain key information in their own
words.

64
00:12:05,376 --> 00:12:16,848
Document the communication barrier, support used, relevant
understanding and agreed plan. Advocacy has improved access without
changing the patient's right to participate.

65
00:12:16,848 --> 00:12:26,688
Separate observation from the patient's report. Patient states is
useful when the information comes from the patient rather than an
independent observation.

66
00:12:26,688 --> 00:12:38,352
Record the barrier, the action offered, the relevant consent and the
outcome. If a referral was offered but declined, say that accurately
rather than charting a completed referral.

67
00:12:38,352 --> 00:12:50,544
Avoid moral labels such as difficult or uncooperative when you can
describe the behaviour and context. For example, the patient declined
the proposed service after its purpose was explained.

68
00:12:50,544 --> 00:13:01,608
Use the patient record for relevant care information. A dispute with a
colleague belongs in the appropriate reporting process; include any
clinically relevant effect on care factually.

69
00:13:01,608 --> 00:13:10,008
Pause and form a response before reading the model reasoning. Your
answer should protect information without becoming hostile to the
neighbour.

70
00:13:10,008 --> 00:13:19,824
A suitable response is: I cannot discuss someone else's health
information. Thank you for offering help; please give us space to
continue our work.

71
00:13:19,824 --> 00:13:31,344
The gift and request create a boundary concern. Do not exchange
information for a favour, and follow the service's gift policy. Record
only what is relevant to the patient's care.

72
00:13:31,344 --> 00:13:42,648
Explain the principles: confidentiality protects trust; autonomy
respects the patient's expressed wish; professional boundaries prevent
private influence over care.
